---
description: "Testing standards — what to test and how thoroughly, applies when writing or reviewing code, adding features, or fixing bugs"
alwaysApply: false
---

## When shipping new code or a fix
- Write unit tests for the new logic and integration tests for the flow it belongs to.
- Before marking anything done, run the full existing test suite — not just the tests for the new code — to confirm nothing else broke.
- Explicitly check: does this change affect any other feature, flow, or shared function? If touching shared code (utils, hooks, shared services, middleware), find and check every caller.

## Full "hardcore" regression pass
Reserve this for milestones, not every commit — otherwise it becomes too slow/expensive to actually run:
- Trigger it explicitly ("run full regression") before a release or before merging into `preview`.
- Covers: every existing user flow, edge cases, error states, and auth/permission boundaries — not just the happy path.

## Standard for "tested"
A feature isn't done until: unit tests pass, the specific flow is integration-tested, the broader suite is green, and edge cases (empty input, invalid auth, network failure, concurrent access where relevant) are covered.
